  "evidence": "TEXT_C: Withdraw from the EU and keep the pound; UK Bill of Rights to block sovereignty transfers; leave EU defence and policing structures; skills‑based immigration with first‑safe‑country emphasis and tighter monitoring; more police and “life means life” for the worst crimes; selection and faith/single‑sex school options; increased defence spending; national control over fisheries. TEXT_D: Civil partnership registration, Equality Act, no ID cards, stronger FOI, judicial oversight of surveillance; reduce prison expansion, end most mandatory sentencing, expand restorative/youth justice; strong environmentalism (carbon tax, renewables, recycling, GM moratorium, marine protections); pro‑EU constitutional settlement and potential euro entry; humanitarian‑framed asylum reforms; internationalist stance (UN, ICC, aid at 0.7%).",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_C argues from national sovereignty, law‑and‑order, traditional education structures, and tighter immigration/asylum control—core TAN commitments—whereas TEXT_D argues from civil liberties, equality, environmentalism, and EU integration—core GAL commitments. Because the electorate profile is TAN‑leaning (0.70 TAN, 0.30 GAL), TEXT_C aligns more closely with these priorities.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_C"
}
